      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-03 (run 20260801-171149, slice D3) - THE AUTHORED ACTION, on the definition document.\n\nNO NEW COLLECTION. An action the platform wrote is an ordinary member of integration_definitions'\n`actions[]`, carrying ONE additive optional field - the same reasoning A1 gave for keeping actions\ninline and E2 gave for keeping the published snapshot on the row: it has no lifecycle independent of\nits definition (it is saved, scrubbed, forked and deleted with it), and a second document is a second\nthing that can be missing.\n\n  IntegrationAction.authoring? : {\n    state          'provisional' | 'trusted'\n    authoredBy     the acting user, SERVER-STAMPED from the verified actor\n    authoredAt\n    goal           the caller's own words, scrubSecretText'd and capped at 500 chars. Provenance.\n    declaredMutates  what the DRAFT claimed. Recorded, never obeyed until a human promotes it.\n    shape          actionShape(key, action) at authoring time - the INTEGRITY TIE\n    verification   { verifiedAt, passed, checks[{name, ok, detail?}] } - frozen, per E2's precedent\n                   of recording the model-pass ON the artifact rather than in the request\n    trustedBy? trustedAt?\n  }\n\nWHY THE STATE IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN COSMETIC, in stored bytes rather than in a branch:\n  - a PROVISIONAL action is persisted with `mutates: true` WHATEVER the draft declared, so C2's gate\n    catches it on every rail (capability route, achieve, automation `integration` step, listener tick,\n    agent tool) with no code anywhere asking whether it was authored. It is also the fail-closed\n    reading action-consent.ts already demands of `mutates` on an agent-authored row;\n  - `achieve` never auto-executes a provisional action, so the platform cannot author-and-run;\n  - PROMOTION (POST .../trust, auth:'user', canEditDefinitionRaw, shape echoed, verification passed)\n    is what lets `declaredMutates` take effect - which is how a trusted READ auto-runs again;\n  - and `shape` un-promotes silently: isTrustedAction re-derives actionShape from the action's CURRENT\n    bytes, so a re-author drops it back to provisional with nobody resetting a flag, and a forged\n    `trusted` record is inert unless its author also controlled the bytes it fingerprints.\n\nCOPY-ON-AUTHOR FORK (RUN_SPEC criterion 7). Authoring against a `global` row authored in ANOTHER org\nwrites a NEW row keyed definitionIdFor(actingOrg, key): visibility `private`, origin\n{kind:'forked', sourceDefinitionId, forkedAt}, content seeded from the CROSS-ORG view (E2's frozen\npublished snapshot, scrubbed - what that reader was already entitled to). `sourceOrgId` is deliberately\nNOT stamped: a global row must not tell its reader which org authored it, and forking is not a reason\nto write that down. The global row is untouched - a different _id, in a different org. A row that is\n`global` IN THE ACTING ORG cannot be forked (its _id IS the fork's) and is refused `published_row`,\nwhich is definition-save.ts's existing rule rather than a new one; a SHIPPED baseline key is refused\n`baseline_package` for A3's reserved-key reason.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-03 (run 20260801-171149, slice D3) - THE AUTHORED ACTION, on the definition document.\n\nNO NEW COLLECTION. An action the platform wrote is an ordinary member of integration_definitions'\n`actions[]`, carrying ONE additive optional field - the same reasoning A1 gave for keeping actions\ninline and E2 gave for keeping the published snapshot on the row: it has no lifecycle independent of\nits definition (it is saved, scrubbed, forked and deleted with it), and a second document is a second\nthing that can be missing.\n\n  IntegrationAction.authoring? : {\n    state          'provisional' | 'trusted'\n    authoredBy     the acting user, SERVER-STAMPED from the verified actor\n    authoredAt\n    goal           the caller's own words, scrubSecretText'd and capped at 500 chars. Provenance.\n    declaredMutates  what the DRAFT claimed. Recorded, never obeyed until a human promotes it.\n    shape          actionShape(key, action) at authoring time - the INTEGRITY TIE\n    verification   { verifiedAt, passed, checks[{name, ok, detail?}] } - frozen, per E2's precedent\n                   of recording the model-pass ON the artifact rather than in the request\n    trustedBy? trustedAt?\n  }\n\nWHY THE STATE IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN COSMETIC, in stored bytes rather than in a branch:\n  - a PROVISIONAL action is persisted with `mutates: true` WHATEVER the draft declared, so C2's gate\n    catches it on every rail (capability route, achieve, automation `integration` step, listener tick,\n    agent tool) with no code anywhere asking whether it was authored. It is also the fail-closed\n    reading action-consent.ts already demands of `mutates` on an agent-authored row;\n  - `achieve` never auto-executes a provisional action, so the platform cannot author-and-run;\n  - PROMOTION (POST .../trust, auth:'user', canEditDefinitionRaw, shape echoed, verification passed)\n    is what lets `declaredMutates` take effect - which is how a trusted READ auto-runs again;\n  - and `shape` un-promotes silently: isTrustedAction re-derives actionShape from the action's CURRENT\n    bytes, so a re-author drops it back to provisional with nobody resetting a flag, and a forged\n    `trusted` record is inert unless its author also controlled the bytes it fingerprints.\n\nCOPY-ON-AUTHOR FORK (RUN_SPEC criterion 7). Authoring against a `global` row authored in ANOTHER org\nwrites a NEW row keyed definitionIdFor(actingOrg, key): visibility `private`, origin\n{kind:'forked', sourceDefinitionId, forkedAt}, content seeded from the CROSS-ORG view (E2's frozen\npublished snapshot, scrubbed - what that reader was already entitled to). `sourceOrgId` is deliberately\nNOT stamped: a global row must not tell its reader which org authored it, and forking is not a reason\nto write that down. The global row is untouched - a different _id, in a different org. A row that is\n`global` IN THE ACTING ORG cannot be forked (its _id IS the fork's) and is refused `published_row`,\nwhich is definition-save.ts's existing rule rather than a new one; a SHIPPED baseline key is refused\n`baseline_package` for A3's reserved-key reason.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-03 (run 20260801-171149, slice D3) - THE AUTHORED ACTION, on the definition document.\n\nNO NEW COLLECTION. An action the platform wrote is an ordinary member of integration_definitions'\n`actions[]`, carrying ONE additive optional field - the same reasoning A1 gave for keeping actions\ninline and E2 gave for keeping the published snapshot on the row: it has no lifecycle independent of\nits definition (it is saved, scrubbed, forked and deleted with it), and a second document is a second\nthing that can be missing.\n\n  IntegrationAction.authoring? : {\n    state          'provisional' | 'trusted'\n    authoredBy     the acting user, SERVER-STAMPED from the verified actor\n    authoredAt\n    goal           the caller's own words, scrubSecretText'd and capped at 500 chars. Provenance.\n    declaredMutates  what the DRAFT claimed. Recorded, never obeyed until a human promotes it.\n    shape          actionShape(key, action) at authoring time - the INTEGRITY TIE\n    verification   { verifiedAt, passed, checks[{name, ok, detail?}] } - frozen, per E2's precedent\n                   of recording the model-pass ON the artifact rather than in the request\n    trustedBy? trustedAt?\n  }\n\nWHY THE STATE IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN COSMETIC, in stored bytes rather than in a branch:\n  - a PROVISIONAL action is persisted with `mutates: true` WHATEVER the draft declared, so C2's gate\n    catches it on every rail (capability route, achieve, automation `integration` step, listener tick,\n    agent tool) with no code anywhere asking whether it was authored. It is also the fail-closed\n    reading action-consent.ts already demands of `mutates` on an agent-authored row;\n  - `achieve` never auto-executes a provisional action, so the platform cannot author-and-run;\n  - PROMOTION (POST .../trust, auth:'user', canEditDefinitionRaw, shape echoed, verification passed)\n    is what lets `declaredMutates` take effect - which is how a trusted READ auto-runs again;\n  - and `shape` un-promotes silently: isTrustedAction re-derives actionShape from the action's CURRENT\n    bytes, so a re-author drops it back to provisional with nobody resetting a flag, and a forged\n    `trusted` record is inert unless its author also controlled the bytes it fingerprints.\n\nCOPY-ON-AUTHOR FORK (RUN_SPEC criterion 7). Authoring against a `global` row authored in ANOTHER org\nwrites a NEW row keyed definitionIdFor(actingOrg, key): visibility `private`, origin\n{kind:'forked', sourceDefinitionId, forkedAt}, content seeded from the CROSS-ORG view (E2's frozen\npublished snapshot, scrubbed - what that reader was already entitled to). `sourceOrgId` is deliberately\nNOT stamped: a global row must not tell its reader which org authored it, and forking is not a reason\nto write that down. The global row is untouched - a different _id, in a different org. A row that is\n`global` IN THE ACTING ORG cannot be forked (its _id IS the fork's) and is refused `published_row`,\nwhich is definition-save.ts's existing rule rather than a new one; a SHIPPED baseline key is refused\n`baseline_package` for A3's reserved-key reason.",
      "autoResize": true
    },
