      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 202608210,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "asbuilt-20260820b-s1r2-evidence-owner-key-and-removal",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 60,
      "y": 12720,
      "strokeColor": "#0e7490",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 1060,
      "height": 660,
      "seed": 202608211,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1755780000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (slice S1 ROUND TWO, corrections to the S1 note above - APPEND-ONLY, so read this\none as authoritative where the two disagree): the evidence row's KEY and its END OF LIFE.\n\nTHE KEY GAINS THE OWNER. `_id` = sha256(JSON([orgId, ownerUserId, integrationKey, actionName])), and\nthe row stores `ownerUserId` beside `orgId`. The note above says the id is (orgId, integrationKey,\nactionName); that was wrong, and measurably so. A CREDENTIAL resolves per (orgId, ownerUserId)\n(integrations/service.ts findConfigForOwner) and an action APPROVAL is keyed on (orgId, userId, ...)\n(integrations/action-consent.ts idFor), so within ONE org an org-visible definition run by two people\nis two different third-party accounts and two different people's client data. Keyed on the org alone:\nthe peer's run wrote the SAME id and destroyed the owner's sample, and integration-achieve.ts's\ntrustAuthoredAction - which reads this collection - let user A promote an action to `trusted` (and so\nauto-runnable by achieve) on a run user B made against B's OWN account. The tenancy claim in the note\nabove therefore now reads: BOTH orgId and ownerUserId are terms of the id, are stored on the row, are\nterms of every query filter and are re-checked on every fetched document; a MIGRATED pre-owner row\n(which carries no ownerUserId) is served to NOBODY rather than to whoever asks first.\n\nTHE ROW HAS AN END OF LIFE, WHICH IT DID NOT BEFORE. The row is durable, has no TTL, and for a\n{kind:'automation'} sample it PINS its run out of the 7-day screenshot sweep. The removal paths are\nnow enumerated FROM THE CODE (the writers of the integration_definitions document) in the store\nheader, and there is exactly ONE: IntegrationDefinitionStore.create(..., onConflict:'replace') - the\nbuilder save (definition-save.ts) and achieve's in-place write. discardEvidenceOfRemovedActions is\ncalled on that branch, beside integration_captured_calls' own discardEvidenceOfRemovedRecipes, and it\ndeletes EVERY OWNER's row for the dropped action while keeping orgId as an exact-match term (an\naction belongs to the definition, so it is dropped for the whole org at once). IntegrationRecipeStore\nis the only other writer of that document and only map()s the existing actions array, so it cannot\ndrop an action; there is NO definition-delete path; retiring a legacy row (global -> org) hides a\ndefinition without dropping an action. Without this the pin never released and the screenshots of an\nauthenticated client-portal session were exempt from retention permanently.\n\nALSO CORRECTED IN THE ROW SHAPE: RunStepEvidence's `title` (which held StepRecord.status - a field\nStepRecord does not have) is now `status`, and ApiCallEvidence.request gains `truncated?` so an\noversized REQUEST body is no longer cut silently.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (slice S1 ROUND TWO, corrections to the S1 note above - APPEND-ONLY, so read this\none as authoritative where the two disagree): the evidence row's KEY and its END OF LIFE.\n\nTHE KEY GAINS THE OWNER. `_id` = sha256(JSON([orgId, ownerUserId, integrationKey, actionName])), and\nthe row stores `ownerUserId` beside `orgId`. The note above says the id is (orgId, integrationKey,\nactionName); that was wrong, and measurably so. A CREDENTIAL resolves per (orgId, ownerUserId)\n(integrations/service.ts findConfigForOwner) and an action APPROVAL is keyed on (orgId, userId, ...)\n(integrations/action-consent.ts idFor), so within ONE org an org-visible definition run by two people\nis two different third-party accounts and two different people's client data. Keyed on the org alone:\nthe peer's run wrote the SAME id and destroyed the owner's sample, and integration-achieve.ts's\ntrustAuthoredAction - which reads this collection - let user A promote an action to `trusted` (and so\nauto-runnable by achieve) on a run user B made against B's OWN account. The tenancy claim in the note\nabove therefore now reads: BOTH orgId and ownerUserId are terms of the id, are stored on the row, are\nterms of every query filter and are re-checked on every fetched document; a MIGRATED pre-owner row\n(which carries no ownerUserId) is served to NOBODY rather than to whoever asks first.\n\nTHE ROW HAS AN END OF LIFE, WHICH IT DID NOT BEFORE. The row is durable, has no TTL, and for a\n{kind:'automation'} sample it PINS its run out of the 7-day screenshot sweep. The removal paths are\nnow enumerated FROM THE CODE (the writers of the integration_definitions document) in the store\nheader, and there is exactly ONE: IntegrationDefinitionStore.create(..., onConflict:'replace') - the\nbuilder save (definition-save.ts) and achieve's in-place write. discardEvidenceOfRemovedActions is\ncalled on that branch, beside integration_captured_calls' own discardEvidenceOfRemovedRecipes, and it\ndeletes EVERY OWNER's row for the dropped action while keeping orgId as an exact-match term (an\naction belongs to the definition, so it is dropped for the whole org at once). IntegrationRecipeStore\nis the only other writer of that document and only map()s the existing actions array, so it cannot\ndrop an action; there is NO definition-delete path; retiring a legacy row (global -> org) hides a\ndefinition without dropping an action. Without this the pin never released and the screenshots of an\nauthenticated client-portal session were exempt from retention permanently.\n\nALSO CORRECTED IN THE ROW SHAPE: RunStepEvidence's `title` (which held StepRecord.status - a field\nStepRecord does not have) is now `status`, and ApiCallEvidence.request gains `truncated?` so an\noversized REQUEST body is no longer cut silently.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (slice S1 ROUND TWO, corrections to the S1 note above - APPEND-ONLY, so read this\none as authoritative where the two disagree): the evidence row's KEY and its END OF LIFE.\n\nTHE KEY GAINS THE OWNER. `_id` = sha256(JSON([orgId, ownerUserId, integrationKey, actionName])), and\nthe row stores `ownerUserId` beside `orgId`. The note above says the id is (orgId, integrationKey,\nactionName); that was wrong, and measurably so. A CREDENTIAL resolves per (orgId, ownerUserId)\n(integrations/service.ts findConfigForOwner) and an action APPROVAL is keyed on (orgId, userId, ...)\n(integrations/action-consent.ts idFor), so within ONE org an org-visible definition run by two people\nis two different third-party accounts and two different people's client data. Keyed on the org alone:\nthe peer's run wrote the SAME id and destroyed the owner's sample, and integration-achieve.ts's\ntrustAuthoredAction - which reads this collection - let user A promote an action to `trusted` (and so\nauto-runnable by achieve) on a run user B made against B's OWN account. The tenancy claim in the note\nabove therefore now reads: BOTH orgId and ownerUserId are terms of the id, are stored on the row, are\nterms of every query filter and are re-checked on every fetched document; a MIGRATED pre-owner row\n(which carries no ownerUserId) is served to NOBODY rather than to whoever asks first.\n\nTHE ROW HAS AN END OF LIFE, WHICH IT DID NOT BEFORE. The row is durable, has no TTL, and for a\n{kind:'automation'} sample it PINS its run out of the 7-day screenshot sweep. The removal paths are\nnow enumerated FROM THE CODE (the writers of the integration_definitions document) in the store\nheader, and there is exactly ONE: IntegrationDefinitionStore.create(..., onConflict:'replace') - the\nbuilder save (definition-save.ts) and achieve's in-place write. discardEvidenceOfRemovedActions is\ncalled on that branch, beside integration_captured_calls' own discardEvidenceOfRemovedRecipes, and it\ndeletes EVERY OWNER's row for the dropped action while keeping orgId as an exact-match term (an\naction belongs to the definition, so it is dropped for the whole org at once). IntegrationRecipeStore\nis the only other writer of that document and only map()s the existing actions array, so it cannot\ndrop an action; there is NO definition-delete path; retiring a legacy row (global -> org) hides a\ndefinition without dropping an action. Without this the pin never released and the screenshots of an\nauthenticated client-portal session were exempt from retention permanently.\n\nALSO CORRECTED IN THE ROW SHAPE: RunStepEvidence's `title` (which held StepRecord.status - a field\nStepRecord does not have) is now `status`, and ApiCallEvidence.request gains `truncated?` so an\noversized REQUEST body is no longer cut silently."
