    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "correction-20260820b-s5-owner-scope-data",
      "containerId": null,
      "x": 60,
      "y": 12685.0,
      "width": 1060,
      "height": 525.0,
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#b45309",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 202608221,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 202608222,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1755740000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20b (S5 verification round two) - THE WIRE SHRINKS BY TWO CODES; THE STORE IS\nUNTOUCHED. Supersedes the refusal-code count in the AS-BUILT note above. Journal: D-S5-1.\n\nSTILL TRUE, AND RE-CHECKED: no new collection, no new stored field, no new scope key. The compose\nrung reads `app_data` through the existing CollectionsEngine and writes nothing.\n\n  THE SCOPE KEY IT READS WITH IS THE ONE THAT ALREADY EXISTED - `usr.<ownerUserId>` - and the fix\n  was to stop pretending it had an app dimension. `sharedScope(appId, ownerUserId)` carries an\n  `appId` on the returned Scope, but `docId` and all six reads bind on `scope.scopeKey` alone, so\n  `Scope.appId` is NEVER part of any query. The binding now uses `ownerSharedScope(actor.userId)`:\n  the ACTING USER'S OWN namespace, from the verified actor and nothing else. It reaches no\n  colleague's rows (a peer's org-visible artifact used to resolve to `usr.<peer>`, opening that\n  peer's whole namespace) and it counts one namespace once (an owner's second app used to make the\n  same rows read as two sources and refuse the call).\n\n  `listCollections(scope)` is unchanged and still returns distinct collection NAMES, never a row -\n  now over exactly one scope per call instead of one per visible artifact.\n\nWHAT CHANGED ON THE WIRE (still additive overall - nothing an older client read has moved):\n  AchieveRefusalCode gains FOUR members, not six. `composed_write_refused` is gone (a write never\n  enters the compose rung, so it cannot be refused by one) and `compose_ambiguous_collection` is\n  gone (one scope per caller means there is no second place for a name to be found in). `code` is a\n  free string on the wire, so neither removal is a schema break; the enum was never published.\n  `candidates` therefore returns to its original meaning - the ACTIONS a goal could have meant -\n  and no longer doubles as a list of artifact ids.\n  `filledArgs` is now produced on `composed` as well as `executed`: an answer that was BOTH\n  model-parametrized and model-narrowed is the one an auditor most needs to see whole.\n\n  activity_logs - unchanged: still exactly one new type, `capability_achieve_compose`, written once\n  per composed answer, recording names and counts only.",
      "rawText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20b (S5 verification round two) - THE WIRE SHRINKS BY TWO CODES; THE STORE IS\nUNTOUCHED. Supersedes the refusal-code count in the AS-BUILT note above. Journal: D-S5-1.\n\nSTILL TRUE, AND RE-CHECKED: no new collection, no new stored field, no new scope key. The compose\nrung reads `app_data` through the existing CollectionsEngine and writes nothing.\n\n  THE SCOPE KEY IT READS WITH IS THE ONE THAT ALREADY EXISTED - `usr.<ownerUserId>` - and the fix\n  was to stop pretending it had an app dimension. `sharedScope(appId, ownerUserId)` carries an\n  `appId` on the returned Scope, but `docId` and all six reads bind on `scope.scopeKey` alone, so\n  `Scope.appId` is NEVER part of any query. The binding now uses `ownerSharedScope(actor.userId)`:\n  the ACTING USER'S OWN namespace, from the verified actor and nothing else. It reaches no\n  colleague's rows (a peer's org-visible artifact used to resolve to `usr.<peer>`, opening that\n  peer's whole namespace) and it counts one namespace once (an owner's second app used to make the\n  same rows read as two sources and refuse the call).\n\n  `listCollections(scope)` is unchanged and still returns distinct collection NAMES, never a row -\n  now over exactly one scope per call instead of one per visible artifact.\n\nWHAT CHANGED ON THE WIRE (still additive overall - nothing an older client read has moved):\n  AchieveRefusalCode gains FOUR members, not six. `composed_write_refused` is gone (a write never\n  enters the compose rung, so it cannot be refused by one) and `compose_ambiguous_collection` is\n  gone (one scope per caller means there is no second place for a name to be found in). `code` is a\n  free string on the wire, so neither removal is a schema break; the enum was never published.\n  `candidates` therefore returns to its original meaning - the ACTIONS a goal could have meant -\n  and no longer doubles as a list of artifact ids.\n  `filledArgs` is now produced on `composed` as well as `executed`: an answer that was BOTH\n  model-parametrized and model-narrowed is the one an auditor most needs to see whole.\n\n  activity_logs - unchanged: still exactly one new type, `capability_achieve_compose`, written once\n  per composed answer, recording names and counts only.",
      "originalText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20b (S5 verification round two) - THE WIRE SHRINKS BY TWO CODES; THE STORE IS\nUNTOUCHED. Supersedes the refusal-code count in the AS-BUILT note above. Journal: D-S5-1.\n\nSTILL TRUE, AND RE-CHECKED: no new collection, no new stored field, no new scope key. The compose\nrung reads `app_data` through the existing CollectionsEngine and writes nothing.\n\n  THE SCOPE KEY IT READS WITH IS THE ONE THAT ALREADY EXISTED - `usr.<ownerUserId>` - and the fix\n  was to stop pretending it had an app dimension. `sharedScope(appId, ownerUserId)` carries an\n  `appId` on the returned Scope, but `docId` and all six reads bind on `scope.scopeKey` alone, so\n  `Scope.appId` is NEVER part of any query. The binding now uses `ownerSharedScope(actor.userId)`:\n  the ACTING USER'S OWN namespace, from the verified actor and nothing else. It reaches no\n  colleague's rows (a peer's org-visible artifact used to resolve to `usr.<peer>`, opening that\n  peer's whole namespace) and it counts one namespace once (an owner's second app used to make the\n  same rows read as two sources and refuse the call).\n\n  `listCollections(scope)` is unchanged and still returns distinct collection NAMES, never a row -\n  now over exactly one scope per call instead of one per visible artifact.\n\nWHAT CHANGED ON THE WIRE (still additive overall - nothing an older client read has moved):\n  AchieveRefusalCode gains FOUR members, not six. `composed_write_refused` is gone (a write never\n  enters the compose rung, so it cannot be refused by one) and `compose_ambiguous_collection` is\n  gone (one scope per caller means there is no second place for a name to be found in). `code` is a\n  free string on the wire, so neither removal is a schema break; the enum was never published.\n  `candidates` therefore returns to its original meaning - the ACTIONS a goal could have meant -\n  and no longer doubles as a list of artifact ids.\n  `filledArgs` is now produced on `composed` as well as `executed`: an answer that was BOTH\n  model-parametrized and model-narrowed is the one an auditor most needs to see whole.\n\n  activity_logs - unchanged: still exactly one new type, `capability_achieve_compose`, written once\n  per composed answer, recording names and counts only."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "correction-20260820c-s4s5-compose-caps-on-wire",
      "containerId": null,
      "x": 60,
      "y": 13410.0,
      "width": 1060,
      "height": 595.0,
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#b45309",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 202608231,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 202608232,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1755830000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20c (S4+S5 verification round three) - THE WIRE GAINS THREE FIELDS AND LOSES\nONE MORE CODE; THE STORE IS STILL UNTOUCHED. Supersedes the AchieveComposition field list and the\nrefusal-code count above. Journal: D-S4-2 / D-S5-2b.\n\nSTILL TRUE, RE-CHECKED A THIRD TIME: NO NEW COLLECTION, NO NEW STORED FIELD, NO NEW SCOPE KEY.\nThe compose rung reads `app_data` through the existing CollectionsEngine binding points; the only\naddition remains listCollections (a `distinct` on the SAME appId filter every other read uses).\n\nWIRE, ADDED (Rule 7 additive; OpenAPI + cortex-cli regenerated in the same commit):\n  AchieveComposition.collectionScanned    number   how many COLLECTION rows the key set was really\n                                                   built from - capped at COMPOSE_MAX_COLLECTION_ROWS\n                                                   (5000), never the number the collection holds.\n  AchieveComposition.collectionTruncated  boolean  TRUE when the collection held MORE rows than were\n                                                   considered: the key set is a PREFIX and `items`\n                                                   may be missing rows a full scan would have kept.\n  AchieveLadderStep.violations            string[] what a DISCARDED rung's answer got wrong. Present\n                                                   only on verdict `refused`.\n\nWHY THE TWO COUNTERS ARE A CORRECTNESS FIELD AND NOT TELEMETRY. COMPOSE_MAX_ITEMS truncates the\nANSWER - the caller asked for rows and got a page of them, and `truncated` says so. The collection\ncap truncates the QUESTION: an action row whose client sits past row 5000 is silently dropped from\nan answer presented as \"the processes of clients under 40\". A subset served as the whole is a WRONG\nanswer, not a partial one, so it is stated on the wire, in the ladder detail (\"matched against the\nfirst 5000 rows of <collection> only\") and on the audit row (an auditor asked later why a row is\nmissing cannot reconstruct that fact from anything else that is recorded).\n\nWIRE, REMOVED: `parametrize_refused`. AchieveRefusalCode is now exactly four ladder codes -\ncompose_refused, compose_unknown_collection, compose_unshaped_result - plus the pre-existing author\ncodes. Every remaining ladder refusal is decided BEFORE anything runs, about a call the caller has\nnot yet made. A rejected argument plan is reported on the ladder and DISCARDED; a failed execute is\nreturned verbatim on the `executed` arm. Neither can take an answer away.\n\nAUDIT ROW (`capability_achieve_compose`), ADDED: collectionScanned, collectionTruncated. Still NOT\nrecorded, unchanged: the goal text, any row, and any value the join keyed on.",
      "rawText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20c (S4+S5 verification round three) - THE WIRE GAINS THREE FIELDS AND LOSES\nONE MORE CODE; THE STORE IS STILL UNTOUCHED. Supersedes the AchieveComposition field list and the\nrefusal-code count above. Journal: D-S4-2 / D-S5-2b.\n\nSTILL TRUE, RE-CHECKED A THIRD TIME: NO NEW COLLECTION, NO NEW STORED FIELD, NO NEW SCOPE KEY.\nThe compose rung reads `app_data` through the existing CollectionsEngine binding points; the only\naddition remains listCollections (a `distinct` on the SAME appId filter every other read uses).\n\nWIRE, ADDED (Rule 7 additive; OpenAPI + cortex-cli regenerated in the same commit):\n  AchieveComposition.collectionScanned    number   how many COLLECTION rows the key set was really\n                                                   built from - capped at COMPOSE_MAX_COLLECTION_ROWS\n                                                   (5000), never the number the collection holds.\n  AchieveComposition.collectionTruncated  boolean  TRUE when the collection held MORE rows than were\n                                                   considered: the key set is a PREFIX and `items`\n                                                   may be missing rows a full scan would have kept.\n  AchieveLadderStep.violations            string[] what a DISCARDED rung's answer got wrong. Present\n                                                   only on verdict `refused`.\n\nWHY THE TWO COUNTERS ARE A CORRECTNESS FIELD AND NOT TELEMETRY. COMPOSE_MAX_ITEMS truncates the\nANSWER - the caller asked for rows and got a page of them, and `truncated` says so. The collection\ncap truncates the QUESTION: an action row whose client sits past row 5000 is silently dropped from\nan answer presented as \"the processes of clients under 40\". A subset served as the whole is a WRONG\nanswer, not a partial one, so it is stated on the wire, in the ladder detail (\"matched against the\nfirst 5000 rows of <collection> only\") and on the audit row (an auditor asked later why a row is\nmissing cannot reconstruct that fact from anything else that is recorded).\n\nWIRE, REMOVED: `parametrize_refused`. AchieveRefusalCode is now exactly four ladder codes -\ncompose_refused, compose_unknown_collection, compose_unshaped_result - plus the pre-existing author\ncodes. Every remaining ladder refusal is decided BEFORE anything runs, about a call the caller has\nnot yet made. A rejected argument plan is reported on the ladder and DISCARDED; a failed execute is\nreturned verbatim on the `executed` arm. Neither can take an answer away.\n\nAUDIT ROW (`capability_achieve_compose`), ADDED: collectionScanned, collectionTruncated. Still NOT\nrecorded, unchanged: the goal text, any row, and any value the join keyed on.",
      "originalText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20c (S4+S5 verification round three) - THE WIRE GAINS THREE FIELDS AND LOSES\nONE MORE CODE; THE STORE IS STILL UNTOUCHED. Supersedes the AchieveComposition field list and the\nrefusal-code count above. Journal: D-S4-2 / D-S5-2b.\n\nSTILL TRUE, RE-CHECKED A THIRD TIME: NO NEW COLLECTION, NO NEW STORED FIELD, NO NEW SCOPE KEY.\nThe compose rung reads `app_data` through the existing CollectionsEngine binding points; the only\naddition remains listCollections (a `distinct` on the SAME appId filter every other read uses).\n\nWIRE, ADDED (Rule 7 additive; OpenAPI + cortex-cli regenerated in the same commit):\n  AchieveComposition.collectionScanned    number   how many COLLECTION rows the key set was really\n                                                   built from - capped at COMPOSE_MAX_COLLECTION_ROWS\n                                                   (5000), never the number the collection holds.\n  AchieveComposition.collectionTruncated  boolean  TRUE when the collection held MORE rows than were\n                                                   considered: the key set is a PREFIX and `items`\n                                                   may be missing rows a full scan would have kept.\n  AchieveLadderStep.violations            string[] what a DISCARDED rung's answer got wrong. Present\n                                                   only on verdict `refused`.\n\nWHY THE TWO COUNTERS ARE A CORRECTNESS FIELD AND NOT TELEMETRY. COMPOSE_MAX_ITEMS truncates the\nANSWER - the caller asked for rows and got a page of them, and `truncated` says so. The collection\ncap truncates the QUESTION: an action row whose client sits past row 5000 is silently dropped from\nan answer presented as \"the processes of clients under 40\". A subset served as the whole is a WRONG\nanswer, not a partial one, so it is stated on the wire, in the ladder detail (\"matched against the\nfirst 5000 rows of <collection> only\") and on the audit row (an auditor asked later why a row is\nmissing cannot reconstruct that fact from anything else that is recorded).\n\nWIRE, REMOVED: `parametrize_refused`. AchieveRefusalCode is now exactly four ladder codes -\ncompose_refused, compose_unknown_collection, compose_unshaped_result - plus the pre-existing author\ncodes. Every remaining ladder refusal is decided BEFORE anything runs, about a call the caller has\nnot yet made. A rejected argument plan is reported on the ladder and DISCARDED; a failed execute is\nreturned verbatim on the `executed` arm. Neither can take an answer away.\n\nAUDIT ROW (`capability_achieve_compose`), ADDED: collectionScanned, collectionTruncated. Still NOT\nrecorded, unchanged: the goal text, any row, and any value the join keyed on."
