      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20e (S4+S5 verification round FIVE) - THE STORE READ IS AN EDGE THAT CAN THROW,\nAND WHAT IT MUST NOT PUT ON THE WIRE. Extends note (d) above. Journal: D-S5-4.\n\nNOTHING IN THE STORE CHANGES, FOR THE FIFTH ROUND RUNNING. app_data keeps its one document shape\n({ _id: \"<scopeKey>::<collection>::<itemId>\", appId, collection, item, _rev }), the compose rung\nstill only READS it, and it still holds no row this rung wrote.\n\nWHAT CHANGES IS THE EDGE INTO IT, which this model previously drew as if it always answered:\n\n    integrations/action-compose (AppCollections seam)\n        |\n        |  server.ts:  read(actor, name) -> collections-engine.list(ownerSharedScope(actor.userId), name)\n        v\n    app_data  -- a MONGO QUERY. Three outcomes, not two:\n                   (1) rows            -> the join runs\n                   (2) no rows         -> { kind: 'unknown_collection' }, composition does not apply\n                   (3) REJECTS         -> dropped connection / timeout / replica-set election\n\nOutcome (3) was undrawn and unhandled. It propagated out of the post-stage as an exception, so a\ncall whose remote had ALREADY answered 200 came back to the caller as a 500 from us, with the rows\nwe were holding discarded. It is now converted into a ladder step and the executed answer, exactly\nlike (2) - see 02-module-map note (e) for the worker/recorder shape that guarantees it.\n\nTHE WIRE SHAPE IS UNCHANGED BY ANY OF THIS (Rule 7): AchieveIntegrationGoalResponse,\nAchieveComposition and AchieveLadderStep are byte-identical to round four. verdict stays the same\nthree values; a store rejection is recorded 'refused', the vocabulary the sibling not-applied paths\nalready use. No field added, none removed, none re-typed - so docs/openapi/cortex.v1.json and the\ncortex-cli types regenerate identically, and the gate is RUN to prove that rather than assumed.\n\nWHAT THE FAILED READ MAY SAY. FIXED TEXT ONLY:\n\n    ladder[].detail = \"the data this goal would have been narrowed by could not be read,\n                       so \\\"<action>\\\" answers unnarrowed\"\n\nThe driver's own message - namespace, replica-set host, query shape - is console.warn'd for an\noperator and never reaches the response. ladder[] is a caller-facing field on a public capability\nendpoint, so a store error's text is an operator's fact, not a caller's. Asserted by requiring the\ninjected failure text to appear nowhere in the body, at the module level and on the wire.\n\nAND THE READ ITSELF IS STILL BOUND TO ONE SCOPE. ownerSharedScope(actor.userId) -> scopeKey\n\"usr.<ownerUserId>\". Every filter in CollectionsEngine binds appId: scope.scopeKey and NONE reads\nscope.appId - which is why the shared unit is the owner and not the app, and which is now asserted\nfrom the engine's source so adding an appId-bound filter reds instead of quietly re-introducing a\nper-app dimension the design assumes away.\n\nORDERING IS PART OF THE DATA CONTRACT HERE, in one narrow place: listCollections sorts, because its\noutput is rendered into a MODEL PROMPT. Unsorted, the same tenant asking the same goal twice is\nasked a different question, since Mongo's distinct gives no order guarantee.\n\nAND THE PREDICATE OVER item.* IS NUMERIC WHERE IT SAYS NUMERIC. lt/lte/gt/gte coerce BOTH sides with\nNumber(). app_data holds whatever an app wrote - a form-entered age is a string, an ERP import writes\nstrings - and the compared value is a model's JSON, which may be \"40\". Without the coercion JS\ncompares lexicographically: over string ages, \"clients under 40\" drops the 9-year-old and admits the\n100-year-old.",
      "rawText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20e (S4+S5 verification round FIVE) - THE STORE READ IS AN EDGE THAT CAN THROW,\nAND WHAT IT MUST NOT PUT ON THE WIRE. Extends note (d) above. Journal: D-S5-4.\n\nNOTHING IN THE STORE CHANGES, FOR THE FIFTH ROUND RUNNING. app_data keeps its one document shape\n({ _id: \"<scopeKey>::<collection>::<itemId>\", appId, collection, item, _rev }), the compose rung\nstill only READS it, and it still holds no row this rung wrote.\n\nWHAT CHANGES IS THE EDGE INTO IT, which this model previously drew as if it always answered:\n\n    integrations/action-compose (AppCollections seam)\n        |\n        |  server.ts:  read(actor, name) -> collections-engine.list(ownerSharedScope(actor.userId), name)\n        v\n    app_data  -- a MONGO QUERY. Three outcomes, not two:\n                   (1) rows            -> the join runs\n                   (2) no rows         -> { kind: 'unknown_collection' }, composition does not apply\n                   (3) REJECTS         -> dropped connection / timeout / replica-set election\n\nOutcome (3) was undrawn and unhandled. It propagated out of the post-stage as an exception, so a\ncall whose remote had ALREADY answered 200 came back to the caller as a 500 from us, with the rows\nwe were holding discarded. It is now converted into a ladder step and the executed answer, exactly\nlike (2) - see 02-module-map note (e) for the worker/recorder shape that guarantees it.\n\nTHE WIRE SHAPE IS UNCHANGED BY ANY OF THIS (Rule 7): AchieveIntegrationGoalResponse,\nAchieveComposition and AchieveLadderStep are byte-identical to round four. verdict stays the same\nthree values; a store rejection is recorded 'refused', the vocabulary the sibling not-applied paths\nalready use. No field added, none removed, none re-typed - so docs/openapi/cortex.v1.json and the\ncortex-cli types regenerate identically, and the gate is RUN to prove that rather than assumed.\n\nWHAT THE FAILED READ MAY SAY. FIXED TEXT ONLY:\n\n    ladder[].detail = \"the data this goal would have been narrowed by could not be read,\n                       so \\\"<action>\\\" answers unnarrowed\"\n\nThe driver's own message - namespace, replica-set host, query shape - is console.warn'd for an\noperator and never reaches the response. ladder[] is a caller-facing field on a public capability\nendpoint, so a store error's text is an operator's fact, not a caller's. Asserted by requiring the\ninjected failure text to appear nowhere in the body, at the module level and on the wire.\n\nAND THE READ ITSELF IS STILL BOUND TO ONE SCOPE. ownerSharedScope(actor.userId) -> scopeKey\n\"usr.<ownerUserId>\". Every filter in CollectionsEngine binds appId: scope.scopeKey and NONE reads\nscope.appId - which is why the shared unit is the owner and not the app, and which is now asserted\nfrom the engine's source so adding an appId-bound filter reds instead of quietly re-introducing a\nper-app dimension the design assumes away.\n\nORDERING IS PART OF THE DATA CONTRACT HERE, in one narrow place: listCollections sorts, because its\noutput is rendered into a MODEL PROMPT. Unsorted, the same tenant asking the same goal twice is\nasked a different question, since Mongo's distinct gives no order guarantee.\n\nAND THE PREDICATE OVER item.* IS NUMERIC WHERE IT SAYS NUMERIC. lt/lte/gt/gte coerce BOTH sides with\nNumber(). app_data holds whatever an app wrote - a form-entered age is a string, an ERP import writes\nstrings - and the compared value is a model's JSON, which may be \"40\". Without the coercion JS\ncompares lexicographically: over string ages, \"clients under 40\" drops the 9-year-old and admits the\n100-year-old.",
      "originalText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-20e (S4+S5 verification round FIVE) - THE STORE READ IS AN EDGE THAT CAN THROW,\nAND WHAT IT MUST NOT PUT ON THE WIRE. Extends note (d) above. Journal: D-S5-4.\n\nNOTHING IN THE STORE CHANGES, FOR THE FIFTH ROUND RUNNING. app_data keeps its one document shape\n({ _id: \"<scopeKey>::<collection>::<itemId>\", appId, collection, item, _rev }), the compose rung\nstill only READS it, and it still holds no row this rung wrote.\n\nWHAT CHANGES IS THE EDGE INTO IT, which this model previously drew as if it always answered:\n\n    integrations/action-compose (AppCollections seam)\n        |\n        |  server.ts:  read(actor, name) -> collections-engine.list(ownerSharedScope(actor.userId), name)\n        v\n    app_data  -- a MONGO QUERY. Three outcomes, not two:\n                   (1) rows            -> the join runs\n                   (2) no rows         -> { kind: 'unknown_collection' }, composition does not apply\n                   (3) REJECTS         -> dropped connection / timeout / replica-set election\n\nOutcome (3) was undrawn and unhandled. It propagated out of the post-stage as an exception, so a\ncall whose remote had ALREADY answered 200 came back to the caller as a 500 from us, with the rows\nwe were holding discarded. It is now converted into a ladder step and the executed answer, exactly\nlike (2) - see 02-module-map note (e) for the worker/recorder shape that guarantees it.\n\nTHE WIRE SHAPE IS UNCHANGED BY ANY OF THIS (Rule 7): AchieveIntegrationGoalResponse,\nAchieveComposition and AchieveLadderStep are byte-identical to round four. verdict stays the same\nthree values; a store rejection is recorded 'refused', the vocabulary the sibling not-applied paths\nalready use. No field added, none removed, none re-typed - so docs/openapi/cortex.v1.json and the\ncortex-cli types regenerate identically, and the gate is RUN to prove that rather than assumed.\n\nWHAT THE FAILED READ MAY SAY. FIXED TEXT ONLY:\n\n    ladder[].detail = \"the data this goal would have been narrowed by could not be read,\n                       so \\\"<action>\\\" answers unnarrowed\"\n\nThe driver's own message - namespace, replica-set host, query shape - is console.warn'd for an\noperator and never reaches the response. ladder[] is a caller-facing field on a public capability\nendpoint, so a store error's text is an operator's fact, not a caller's. Asserted by requiring the\ninjected failure text to appear nowhere in the body, at the module level and on the wire.\n\nAND THE READ ITSELF IS STILL BOUND TO ONE SCOPE. ownerSharedScope(actor.userId) -> scopeKey\n\"usr.<ownerUserId>\". Every filter in CollectionsEngine binds appId: scope.scopeKey and NONE reads\nscope.appId - which is why the shared unit is the owner and not the app, and which is now asserted\nfrom the engine's source so adding an appId-bound filter reds instead of quietly re-introducing a\nper-app dimension the design assumes away.\n\nORDERING IS PART OF THE DATA CONTRACT HERE, in one narrow place: listCollections sorts, because its\noutput is rendered into a MODEL PROMPT. Unsorted, the same tenant asking the same goal twice is\nasked a different question, since Mongo's distinct gives no order guarantee.\n\nAND THE PREDICATE OVER item.* IS NUMERIC WHERE IT SAYS NUMERIC. lt/lte/gt/gte coerce BOTH sides with\nNumber(). app_data holds whatever an app wrote - a form-entered age is a string, an ERP import writes\nstrings - and the compared value is a model's JSON, which may be \"40\". Without the coercion JS\ncompares lexicographically: over string ages, \"clients under 40\" drops the 9-year-old and admits the\n100-year-old."
    },
