      "text": "CORRECTION 2026-08-21f (S4+S5 verification round SIX) - THE LISTER ANSWERS FIELDS, AND A GUESSED\nNAME STOPS BEING A NARROWED ANSWER. Extends notes (c), (d) and (e). Journal: D-S5-5.\n\nTHE SHAPE THAT CHANGED (a SEAM shape; nothing on the wire moved)\n  BEFORE  AppCollections.list(actor) -> string[]              (collection names)\n  AFTER   AppCollections.list(actor) -> { name, fields }[]    (names + each collection's row keys)\n  bound to CollectionsEngine.listCollectionFields, which replaces listCollections. One aggregation:\n    $match { appId: scope.scopeKey } -> $project the item's keys -> $unwind -> $group by collection\n  SAME single query-binding point as every other read in the engine. The field set is EXACT, not\n  sampled: a sampled union is a SUBSET, so a legitimate field the sample missed would be refused and\n  the caller would lose a narrowing they were entitled to.\n\nTHE OTHER FIELD SET HAS NO STORED SHAPE AT ALL, and that is why the rung had to move below the\nexecute to get it. IntegrationAction.returnSchema was considered and REJECTED as the source: it is\nunvalidated documentation (definitions.ts lifts it off a config.json verbatim), it is absent on\nalmost every shipped action, and when present it describes the ENVELOPE - whose top-level keys are\nnot the row's. The only exact answer is fieldsOf(rows): the sorted UNION of keys over the rows the\naction really returned.\n\nWHAT THE OLD SHAPE PUT ON THE WIRE, and why no field of it was wrong\n  200 {\n        \"outcome\": \"composed\",\n        \"items\": [],                                  <- a SHORTER LIST, or an empty one\n        \"composition\": { \"collection\": \"clients\",\n                         \"where\": { \"field\": \"age\", \"op\": \"lt\", \"value\": 40 },\n                         \"matchedCollectionRows\": 0, \"matched\": 0,\n                         \"collectionTruncated\": false }\n      }\n  Every field is accurate about what the code DID. Nothing anywhere says the field named does not\n  exist on the caller's own rows, and that is the only fact that mattered.\n\nWHAT IT PUTS ON THE WIRE NOW - the executed arm, whole, with the reason beside it\n  200 { \"outcome\": \"executed\",\n        \"result\": { \"success\": true, \"data\": { \"processos\": [ ...all of them... ] } },\n        \"ladder\": [ { \"rung\": \"compose\", \"verdict\": \"refused\",\n                      \"violations\": [ \"\\\"where.field\\\": \\\"age\\\" is not a field of \\\"clients\\\"\n                                       (createdAt, id, idade, nome, updatedAt)\" ] },\n                    { \"rung\": \"reuse\", \"verdict\": \"taken\" } ] }\n  The offered set travels IN the violation. No schema field was added, removed or re-typed:\n  ladder[].violations already existed, so the openapi + cortex-cli regeneration is byte-identical.\n\nTWO MORE VALUES ON THIS MAP THAT WERE NOT WHAT THEY CLAIMED\n  where.value accepted ANY JSON - an object or an array reached matchesSimpleQuery, where the\n  orderings read Number({...}) as NaN, eq/neq compare by reference against a value that arrived over\n  JSON (eq never matches, neq always does), and the string ops stringify to \"[object Object]\". It is\n  now a scalar-or-null check of its own, the sibling rung's rule (action-parametrize.ts check 5).\n  composition.truncated used >= at the boundary under mutation and nothing noticed: a join matching\n  EXACTLY COMPOSE_MAX_ITEMS rows would claim the caller did not receive part of an answer they\n  received whole. Pinned as a PAIR, the way the sibling constant already was.\n\nTENANCY NOTE (Rule 5). The prompt now carries more of the tenant's own metadata than it did, so\n\"org B tracks salaries\" is a second disclosable fact beside \"org B runs a payroll app\".\nachieve-compose-isolation.test.ts tests 1, 4 and 5 assert the absence of a peer's FIELD names as\nwell as of their collection names.",
      "rawText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-21f (S4+S5 verification round SIX) - THE LISTER ANSWERS FIELDS, AND A GUESSED\nNAME STOPS BEING A NARROWED ANSWER. Extends notes (c), (d) and (e). Journal: D-S5-5.\n\nTHE SHAPE THAT CHANGED (a SEAM shape; nothing on the wire moved)\n  BEFORE  AppCollections.list(actor) -> string[]              (collection names)\n  AFTER   AppCollections.list(actor) -> { name, fields }[]    (names + each collection's row keys)\n  bound to CollectionsEngine.listCollectionFields, which replaces listCollections. One aggregation:\n    $match { appId: scope.scopeKey } -> $project the item's keys -> $unwind -> $group by collection\n  SAME single query-binding point as every other read in the engine. The field set is EXACT, not\n  sampled: a sampled union is a SUBSET, so a legitimate field the sample missed would be refused and\n  the caller would lose a narrowing they were entitled to.\n\nTHE OTHER FIELD SET HAS NO STORED SHAPE AT ALL, and that is why the rung had to move below the\nexecute to get it. IntegrationAction.returnSchema was considered and REJECTED as the source: it is\nunvalidated documentation (definitions.ts lifts it off a config.json verbatim), it is absent on\nalmost every shipped action, and when present it describes the ENVELOPE - whose top-level keys are\nnot the row's. The only exact answer is fieldsOf(rows): the sorted UNION of keys over the rows the\naction really returned.\n\nWHAT THE OLD SHAPE PUT ON THE WIRE, and why no field of it was wrong\n  200 {\n        \"outcome\": \"composed\",\n        \"items\": [],                                  <- a SHORTER LIST, or an empty one\n        \"composition\": { \"collection\": \"clients\",\n                         \"where\": { \"field\": \"age\", \"op\": \"lt\", \"value\": 40 },\n                         \"matchedCollectionRows\": 0, \"matched\": 0,\n                         \"collectionTruncated\": false }\n      }\n  Every field is accurate about what the code DID. Nothing anywhere says the field named does not\n  exist on the caller's own rows, and that is the only fact that mattered.\n\nWHAT IT PUTS ON THE WIRE NOW - the executed arm, whole, with the reason beside it\n  200 { \"outcome\": \"executed\",\n        \"result\": { \"success\": true, \"data\": { \"processos\": [ ...all of them... ] } },\n        \"ladder\": [ { \"rung\": \"compose\", \"verdict\": \"refused\",\n                      \"violations\": [ \"\\\"where.field\\\": \\\"age\\\" is not a field of \\\"clients\\\"\n                                       (createdAt, id, idade, nome, updatedAt)\" ] },\n                    { \"rung\": \"reuse\", \"verdict\": \"taken\" } ] }\n  The offered set travels IN the violation. No schema field was added, removed or re-typed:\n  ladder[].violations already existed, so the openapi + cortex-cli regeneration is byte-identical.\n\nTWO MORE VALUES ON THIS MAP THAT WERE NOT WHAT THEY CLAIMED\n  where.value accepted ANY JSON - an object or an array reached matchesSimpleQuery, where the\n  orderings read Number({...}) as NaN, eq/neq compare by reference against a value that arrived over\n  JSON (eq never matches, neq always does), and the string ops stringify to \"[object Object]\". It is\n  now a scalar-or-null check of its own, the sibling rung's rule (action-parametrize.ts check 5).\n  composition.truncated used >= at the boundary under mutation and nothing noticed: a join matching\n  EXACTLY COMPOSE_MAX_ITEMS rows would claim the caller did not receive part of an answer they\n  received whole. Pinned as a PAIR, the way the sibling constant already was.\n\nTENANCY NOTE (Rule 5). The prompt now carries more of the tenant's own metadata than it did, so\n\"org B tracks salaries\" is a second disclosable fact beside \"org B runs a payroll app\".\nachieve-compose-isolation.test.ts tests 1, 4 and 5 assert the absence of a peer's FIELD names as\nwell as of their collection names.",
      "originalText": "CORRECTION 2026-08-21f (S4+S5 verification round SIX) - THE LISTER ANSWERS FIELDS, AND A GUESSED\nNAME STOPS BEING A NARROWED ANSWER. Extends notes (c), (d) and (e). Journal: D-S5-5.\n\nTHE SHAPE THAT CHANGED (a SEAM shape; nothing on the wire moved)\n  BEFORE  AppCollections.list(actor) -> string[]              (collection names)\n  AFTER   AppCollections.list(actor) -> { name, fields }[]    (names + each collection's row keys)\n  bound to CollectionsEngine.listCollectionFields, which replaces listCollections. One aggregation:\n    $match { appId: scope.scopeKey } -> $project the item's keys -> $unwind -> $group by collection\n  SAME single query-binding point as every other read in the engine. The field set is EXACT, not\n  sampled: a sampled union is a SUBSET, so a legitimate field the sample missed would be refused and\n  the caller would lose a narrowing they were entitled to.\n\nTHE OTHER FIELD SET HAS NO STORED SHAPE AT ALL, and that is why the rung had to move below the\nexecute to get it. IntegrationAction.returnSchema was considered and REJECTED as the source: it is\nunvalidated documentation (definitions.ts lifts it off a config.json verbatim), it is absent on\nalmost every shipped action, and when present it describes the ENVELOPE - whose top-level keys are\nnot the row's. The only exact answer is fieldsOf(rows): the sorted UNION of keys over the rows the\naction really returned.\n\nWHAT THE OLD SHAPE PUT ON THE WIRE, and why no field of it was wrong\n  200 {\n        \"outcome\": \"composed\",\n        \"items\": [],                                  <- a SHORTER LIST, or an empty one\n        \"composition\": { \"collection\": \"clients\",\n                         \"where\": { \"field\": \"age\", \"op\": \"lt\", \"value\": 40 },\n                         \"matchedCollectionRows\": 0, \"matched\": 0,\n                         \"collectionTruncated\": false }\n      }\n  Every field is accurate about what the code DID. Nothing anywhere says the field named does not\n  exist on the caller's own rows, and that is the only fact that mattered.\n\nWHAT IT PUTS ON THE WIRE NOW - the executed arm, whole, with the reason beside it\n  200 { \"outcome\": \"executed\",\n        \"result\": { \"success\": true, \"data\": { \"processos\": [ ...all of them... ] } },\n        \"ladder\": [ { \"rung\": \"compose\", \"verdict\": \"refused\",\n                      \"violations\": [ \"\\\"where.field\\\": \\\"age\\\" is not a field of \\\"clients\\\"\n                                       (createdAt, id, idade, nome, updatedAt)\" ] },\n                    { \"rung\": \"reuse\", \"verdict\": \"taken\" } ] }\n  The offered set travels IN the violation. No schema field was added, removed or re-typed:\n  ladder[].violations already existed, so the openapi + cortex-cli regeneration is byte-identical.\n\nTWO MORE VALUES ON THIS MAP THAT WERE NOT WHAT THEY CLAIMED\n  where.value accepted ANY JSON - an object or an array reached matchesSimpleQuery, where the\n  orderings read Number({...}) as NaN, eq/neq compare by reference against a value that arrived over\n  JSON (eq never matches, neq always does), and the string ops stringify to \"[object Object]\". It is\n  now a scalar-or-null check of its own, the sibling rung's rule (action-parametrize.ts check 5).\n  composition.truncated used >= at the boundary under mutation and nothing noticed: a join matching\n  EXACTLY COMPOSE_MAX_ITEMS rows would claim the caller did not receive part of an answer they\n  received whole. Pinned as a PAIR, the way the sibling constant already was.\n\nTENANCY NOTE (Rule 5). The prompt now carries more of the tenant's own metadata than it did, so\n\"org B tracks salaries\" is a second disclosable fact beside \"org B runs a payroll app\".\nachieve-compose-isolation.test.ts tests 1, 4 and 5 assert the absence of a peer's FIELD names as\nwell as of their collection names.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ]
